                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\theta",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " using the perimeter and area, then solve the resulting quadratic.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}r\\theta+2r&=28+6\\pi\\\\\\theta&=\\frac{28+6\\pi-2r}{r}\\\\42\\pi&=\\frac12r^2\\left(\\frac{28+6\\pi-2r}{r}\\right)\\\\r^2-(14+3\\pi)r+42\\pi&=0\\\\(r-14)(r-3\\pi)&=0\\\\r&=14\\text{ or }3\\pi\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $r=14$ cm, $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{7}$ radians.\n\nFor $r=3\\pi$ cm, $\\theta=\\dfrac{28}{3\\pi}$ radians.\n\nBoth pairs satisfy the stated perimeter and area, with $0<\\theta<2\\pi$.",
